# GATBOX: keeps the hdparm package's own udev rule from running (installed by gatbox-bootstrap.sh).
#
# This file has the same name as the package's /usr/lib/udev/rules.d/85-hdparm.rules, and a rules file in
# /etc/udev/rules.d shadows a same-named one in /usr/lib, so that rule never loads. It would otherwise run
# /usr/lib/udev/hdparm on every sd? disk as it appears, and that helper sets a power-management level (-B254) on any
# drive that reports APM. It skips USB drives only when their path starts with pci-*-usb-, and on a Pi 5 a USB path
# starts with platform-xhci-hcd, so it would not skip them. A drive in the dock should be touched only when a disk
# job or `sudo hdparm -I` asks. The hdparm tool itself is installed and works as usual.
